Modifying the USS Iowa (BB-61)—whether you are reference-modeling it, building a custom gaming mod (like for Modern Warships), or conceptualizing a modern hybrid electric drive retrofitted into its historic hull spaces—the physical limitations of an Iowa-class engine room are notoriously brutal. [1] 
The original ship layout is an incredibly dense maze of Babcock & Wilcox boilers and General Electric/Westinghouse steam turbines. There is virtually zero vertical clearance or wide-open floor space. [2, 3] 
If a standard Medium Voltage (MV) marine motor or switchgear layout is throwing an error or clashing because it is "too big," you have to pivot to ultra-compact military or specialized configurations to fit the spaces:
## 1. Shift to Permanent Magnet Technology (Motors)
If you are trying to fit an MV motor down in the shaft lines or engine rooms, standard induction motors (even compact ones) are too tall and wide.

* You need to drop in a Siemens FLEX PM (Permanent Magnet) motor.
* Permanent magnet motors provide the exact same megawatt output as massive traditional motors but cut the physical footprint and volume by up to 40% to 50%. They are explicitly designed for highly restricted naval vessels.

## 2. Move the MV Switchgear to 8DJH Compact (GIS)
The USS Iowa originally generated 450V AC power via its Westinghouse turbo-generators. If you are stepping up to Medium Voltage (e.g., 6.6 kV or 11 kV) for modern defense systems, a standard air-insulated switchboard will physically hit the overhead bulkheads. [4] 
Siemens 8DJH Compact Gas-Insulated Switchgear: You must utilize Siemens 8DJH Compact Gas-Insulated Switchgear. Because it uses sealed gas insulation instead of air gaps, the panels can be placed directly against the armored bulkheads, saving up to 75% of the floor space of a traditional shipboard switchboard layout.
